      <li><a href="#shore">Shore roles that use the s</a></li>
      <li><a href="#cost">A cost and risk worksheet </a></li>
      <li><a href="#money">Money</a></li>
      <li><a href="#checkpoint">The 4-Checkpoint Protocol</a></li>
      <li><a href="#gate3">The 3 Gates</a></li>
      <li><a href="#mistakes">Expensive mistakes</a></li>
      <li><a href="#next-step">What to do next</a></li>
      <li><a href="#faq">FAQs</a></li>
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="verdict">The verdict: verify approvals and costs in writing before any payment</h2>

  <p>
    Prepare in stages and keep proof. Employers and clients look for evidence, not claims.
  </p>
  <p>
    Check what your target role actually asks for by reading notices, then practise that version of the skill.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Type of graduate</th>
          <th>Our read</th>
          <th>Why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Type of graduate"><strong>Verifying approval in writing</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Our read">Strong</td>
          <td data-label="Why">It protects money.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Type of graduate"><strong>Plan B with shore roles</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Our read">Strong</td>
          <td data-label="Why">It reduces risk.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Type of graduate"><strong>Speaking to past students</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Our read">Good</td>
          <td data-label="Why">It gives honest views.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Type of graduate"><strong>Paying on verbal promises</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Our read">Risky</td>
          <td data-label="Why">Money can be lost.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="evidence">What can be said with confidence, and what cannot</h2>

  <p>
    
  </p>
