  <h2 id="sales">Who pays for these skills, and how they judge you</h2>

  <p>
    Shipping firms and ports pay for safety, reliability and discipline. They judge you by records, certificates and references.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first basic skill is checking approvals and costs in writing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and BSc nautical science: which tasks are under pressure</h2>

  <p>
    AI and automation affect planning and documentation on ships and ashore. Routine paperwork shrinks first.
  </p>
  <p>
    Seamanship, safety judgment and accountability hold value. A staged path helps: now, use tools for planning; later, own the safety decisions.
  </p>

  <h2 id="better">Alternatives that can beat a general BSc nautical science path</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Alternative</th>
          <th>Why it can beat it</th>
          <th>Catch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Merchant navy through other routes</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Different entry</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Different rules</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Marine engineering route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Engine side</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Needs engineering</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Logistics route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Shore based</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Less sea pay</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="learn">How to learn BSc nautical science cheaply, and what proof counts</h2>

  <p>
    BSc nautical science is learned by doing real work, so the cheapest route is also the best. A paid course is worth it only when it gives feedback and finished work that you cannot get free.
  </p>
  <h3>Free and low-cost routes first</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Free NPTEL and SWAYAM lessons.</li>
    <li>Free YouTube lessons and public documentation.</li>
    <li>Free practice datasets, field notes or open software.</li>
    <li>A senior or teacher who reviews your sample.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to judge a paid course</h3>
  <p>
    Judge each course, not the platform. Ask what you will build, who reviews it, which role graduates reached, how current the material is and what the refund terms are, in writing.
  </p>
  <h3>Your week, and a first small test</h3>
  <p>
    A starting plan is about ten hours a week: three of lessons and seven of practice on one project. Do not pay for an expensive programme before this habit holds.
  </p>
  <p>
    This week, list five questions about approval, cost and placement, and ask two past students for honest answers.
  </p>
  <h3>Building a high-income skill portfolio beside the credential</h3>
  <p>
    A degree has a place, and the weak spot is relying on it alone. A skill mix of one core skill, a second skill, communication, a niche and a clear offer builds a high-income skill portfolio, and a niche service can bring an earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <h2 id="verify">What to verify before paying any fee</h2>

  <p>
    This field has many agents and unclear claims. Verify on official maritime authority pages and ask for everything in writing.
  </p>
